81	     * This class guesses harmonic coefficients from a sample.
82	     * <p>The algorithm used to guess the coefficients is as follows:</p>
83	     *
84	     * <p>We know f (t) at some sampling points t<sub>i</sub> and want to find a,
85	     * &omega; and &phi; such that f (t) = a cos (&omega; t + &phi;).
86	     * </p>
87	     *
88	     * <p>From the analytical expression, we can compute two primitives :
89	     * <pre>
90	     *     If2  (t) = &int; f<sup>2</sup>  = a<sup>2</sup> &times; [t + S (t)] / 2
91	     *     If'2 (t) = &int; f'<sup>2</sup> = a<sup>2</sup> &omega;<sup>2</sup> &times; [t - S (t)] / 2
92	     *     where S (t) = sin (2 (&omega; t + &phi;)) / (2 &omega;)
93	     * </pre>
94	     * </p>
95	     *
96	     * <p>We can remove S between these expressions :
97	     * <pre>
98	     *     If'2 (t) = a<sup>2</sup> &omega;<sup>2</sup> t - &omega;<sup>2</sup> If2 (t)
99	     * </pre>
100	     * </p>
101	     *
102	     * <p>The preceding expression shows that If'2 (t) is a linear
103	     * combination of both t and If2 (t): If'2 (t) = A &times; t + B &times; If2 (t)
104	     * </p>
105	     *
106	     * <p>From the primitive, we can deduce the same form for definite
107	     * integrals between t<sub>1</sub> and t<sub>i</sub> for each t<sub>i</sub> :
108	     * <pre>
109	     *   If2 (t<sub>i</sub>) - If2 (t<sub>1</sub>) = A &times; (t<sub>i</sub> - t<sub>1</sub>) + B &times; (If2 (t<sub>i</sub>) - If2 (t<sub>1</sub>))
110	     * </pre>
111	     * </p>
112	     *
113	     * <p>We can find the coefficients A and B that best fit the sample
114	     * to this linear expression by computing the definite integrals for
115	     * each sample points.
116	     * </p>
117	     *
118	     * <p>For a bilinear expression z (x<sub>i</sub>, y<sub>i</sub>) = A &times; x<sub>i</sub> + B &times; y<sub>i</sub>, the
119	     * coefficients A and B that minimize a least square criterion
120	     * &sum; (z<sub>i</sub> - z (x<sub>i</sub>, y<sub>i</sub>))<sup>2</sup> are given by these expressions:</p>
121	     * <pre>
122	     *
123	     *         &sum;y<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> &sum;x<sub>i</sub>z<sub>i</sub> - &sum;x<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> &sum;y<sub>i</sub>z<sub>i</sub>
124	     *     A = ------------------------
125	     *         &sum;x<sub>i</sub>x<sub>i</sub> &sum;y<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> - &sum;x<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> &sum;x<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub>
126	     *
127	     *         &sum;x<sub>i</sub>x<sub>i</sub> &sum;y<sub>i</sub>z<sub>i</sub> - &sum;x<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> &sum;x<sub>i</sub>z<sub>i</sub>
128	     *     B = ------------------------
129	     *         &sum;x<sub>i</sub>x<sub>i</sub> &sum;y<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> - &sum;x<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub> &sum;x<sub>i</sub>y<sub>i</sub>
130	     * </pre>
131	     * </p>
132	     *
133	     *
134	     * <p>In fact, we can assume both a and &omega; are positive and
135	     * compute them directly, knowing that A = a<sup>2</sup> &omega;<sup>2</sup> and that
136	     * B = - &omega;<sup>2</sup>. The complete algorithm is therefore:</p>
137	     * <pre>
