158	     *
159	     * </pre>
160	     * </p>
161	     *
162	     * <p>Once we know &omega;, we can compute:
163	     * <pre>
164	     *    fc = &omega; f (t) cos (&omega; t) - f' (t) sin (&omega; t)
165	     *    fs = &omega; f (t) sin (&omega; t) + f' (t) cos (&omega; t)
166	     * </pre>
167	     * </p>
168	     *
169	     * <p>It appears that <code>fc = a &omega; cos (&phi;)</code> and
170	     * <code>fs = -a &omega; sin (&phi;)</code>, so we can use these
171	     * expressions to compute &phi;. The best estimate over the sample is
172	     * given by averaging these expressions.
173	     * </p>
174	     *
175	     * <p>Since integrals and means are involved in the preceding
176	     * estimations, these operations run in O(n) time, where n is the
